  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTuesday morning, ensembles show three 500-mb features that drive \nweather across the forecast area: a high across the Southeastern \nUnited States, a trough across the Western United States, and broad \ntroughing across portions of Southeast Canada. A shortwave lifting \nacross the Midwest would be associated with northeasterly surface \nflow across the forecast area, setting the stage for cooler \nconditions through the duration of the long term period. Surface \nhighs Tuesday are currently forecast in the mid to upper 70s, but \nlower percentiles of the NBM show that highs as low as the mid to \nupper 60s can't be ruled out. Temperatures are favored to remain \nlower than recent weeks Wednesday and Thursday even though southerly \nflow would generally be favored between the high across the \nsoutheast and trough across the Western United States. This would be \ncaused by continued easterly to northeasterly surface flow from a \nstrong surface high to the northeast of the forecast region, and \npotential cloud cover and precipitation from convergence across \nKansas. Currently, surface highs are forecast in the mid 70s to \nlower 80s Wednesday and Thursday. Temperatures could reach a little \nhigher than the current forecast depending on the northerly extent \nof the southerly flow, and whether any solar heating will be allowed \nto develop. \n\nAs mentioned before, precipitation is favored to occur Tuesday \nthrough Thursday. Tuesday, all areas in the forecast area have at \nleast a 25% chance or greater of receiving more than 0.1 inches of \nrain according to NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance, with areas along \nand east of a line from Oberlin, KS to Tribune, KS showing a 50-75% \nchance. Most of this precipitation is expected to be along the cold \nfront in the area, and is not favored to be convective in \nnature, as LREF guidance shows an 80% chance or higher across \nthe forecast region that surface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric \ninstability) will remain below 1000 J/kg. Convergence across \nKansas from southerly flow meeting easterly flow would continue \nto support precipitation Wednesday and Thursday. NBM 24 hr \nguidance shows Wednesday to be the better day for precipitation,\nwith a 50-75% chance across nearly the entire forecast region \nfor greater than 0.1 inches of rain. Also, there may be a \nbetter chance for convective activity Wednesday, as LREF \nguidance shows a 40-45% chance of surface CAPE greater than 1000\nJ/kg between Kansas Highways 25 and 27. GFS and EC soundings \nshow sfc-6km shear between 20 and 35 kts, which would suggest \nthat most thunderstorms would be sub-severe, with perhaps a \nstorm or two that could produce winds up to 60 mph. Confidence \nin severe storms occurring Wednesday is around 5% or less. \nPrecipitation is not favored to be convective Thursday, as \ninstability looks to decrease again. \n\nThe trough across the Western United States looks to be slowly \nmoving toward the forecast area during the duration of the long \nterm, though it is not entirely certain when the trough will move \nin. Ensemble guidance mutes this feature by Friday morning. \nAdditionally, GFS and EC deterministics continue to show this wave \nas a weaker one, which would indicate most of the uncertainty is in \nassociation with the timing of the trough. As long as the trough \nremains west of the forecast area, conditions similar to Tuesday \nthrough Thursday would be more likely. However, conditions may cool \na little more once the wave moves over the region and northerly flow \nreestablishes itself. NBM 25th percentile solutions and lower show \nhighs in the mid 70s or lower Friday and Saturday in association \nwith this trough moving overhead.",
